\subsection{Review the whole log in batch mode}

The opening step establishes the whole before the parts: the entire
history as plain terminal output, which the later tag steps will then
subdivide.
Its object of learning is that the log is readable at all, including
the pacing cue: [[Time since last run]] makes quick retries and long
pauses visible.
The expert extra is that this review run records itself---reflection
is part of the story the log tells---which reframes [[play]] from a
passive reader to a participant.

<<shared step: review the whole log in batch mode>>=
# Review the whole log in batch mode

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+play\b[^\n]*(?:-b\b|--batch\b)'
hint: Start with `learnlog play -b` so you can read the whole log.
```

Use a learnlog project that already contains a few runs.
Begin with `learnlog play -b` to see the full history as plain terminal
output.
Each run after the first also shows `Time since last run`, which makes quick
retries and longer pauses visible during playback.
<<novice step: review the whole log in batch mode>>=
<<shared step: review the whole log in batch mode>>
<<expert step: review the whole log in batch mode>>=
<<shared step: review the whole log in batch mode>>

This review run is itself part of the story: `play` and `list` are the
only learnlog commands that record themselves, so your log also shows
when you went back and read it.
Batch playback records a view of each commit it prints.
@

\subsection{Add a memorable tag}

The step's object of learning is that a milestone can carry a name
the learner chooses, and that the name marks a \emph{start}: it
covers the tagged run and everything after it, until the next tag
(\cref{sec:metrics-tags}).
The expert extra says what the name is---a lightweight Git tag on the
run's commit---and why tagging leaves no trace of its own: [[tag]] is
not a recorded command, so the learner can tag their most recent run
without creating new history behind it.

<<shared step: add a memorable tag>>=
# Add a memorable tag

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+tag\s+(?!-)\S+'
hint: Tag the current commit with a short name such as `lab1`.
```

Start a new piece of work, run it once, and name that starting
point, for example with `learnlog tag lab1`.
The tag covers this run and everything you run after it, until your
next tag.
<<novice step: add a memorable tag>>=
<<shared step: add a memorable tag>>
<<expert step: add a memorable tag>>=
<<shared step: add a memorable tag>>

The tag is a lightweight Git tag in the hidden `.learnlog/` repository,
pointing at the run's commit.
Unlike `play`, `tag` does not record itself, which is what makes it
possible to name your own most recent run without creating new history
behind it.

`learnlog metrics --since`, `--by tag` and `learnlog analyse` all
read the tag as a start marker, the way `git describe` does---which
is why you tag when you start a milestone, not when you finish one.
@

\subsection{List the available tags}

A small confirmation step: tags are enumerable, so the learner can
verify the marker exists before relying on it.
Only the hint diverges---the expert's names the hidden repository the
tags live in, the novice's does not---so the checks and prose are one
shared chunk.

<<checks: list the available tags>>=
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+tag\b[^\n]*(?:-l\b|--list\b)'
<<novice step: list the available tags>>=
# List the available tags

```tutorial-step
<<checks: list the available tags>>
hint: Run `learnlog tag -l` to list your tags.
```

<<instruction: list the available tags>>
<<expert step: list the available tags>>=
# List the available tags

```tutorial-step
<<checks: list the available tags>>
hint: Run `learnlog tag -l` to list the tags in the `.learnlog/` repository.
```

